    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 3.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-median-and-quartiles-grouped">
        <title>Median and Quartiles of Grouped Data</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> for grouped data the positions are <m>n/4</m>, <m>n/2</m> and <m>3n/4</m>; inside a class the values are assumed to be spread evenly from the lower class boundary.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> estimate the median and quartiles of grouped data with the formula.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> use the formula backwards to find a missing frequency.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners estimate the journey time of the 10th learner without the raw data, and so discover interpolation inside a class.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Use the table from the activity in the previous section. Give each group a paper strip of <m>9</m> equal squares for the class 15–24. Ask: <q>If the 9 learners in this class are spread evenly, where is the 6th?</q></p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-where-is-the-10th-learner">
            <title>Where Is the 10th Learner?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The table repeats the journey times to school, in minutes (to the nearest minute), of <m>40</m> learners in Nakuru from <xref ref="act-getting-to-school"/>, with the cumulative frequencies.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-where-is-the-10th-learner">
                    <title>Journey Times of 40 Learners</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Time (minutes)</cell><cell>5–14</cell><cell>15–24</cell><cell>25–34</cell><cell>35–44</cell><cell>45–54</cell><cell>55–64</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>13</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>2</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Cumulative frequency (cf)</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>13</cell><cell>26</cell><cell>34</cell><cell>38</cell><cell>40</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Imagine the 40 learners in order of journey time. In which class is the 10th learner? The 20th? The 30th?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>15–24 (the cumulative frequency first reaches 10 there), 25–34 and 35–44.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>How many learners come before the class 15–24? So which learner in that class is the 10th learner overall?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>4</m> learners, so the 10th learner is the 6th of the <m>9</m> in the class.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>The class 15–24 really runs from <m>14.5</m> to <m>24.5</m> minutes. Assume its <m>9</m> learners are spread evenly across it. Estimate the 10th learner's time, to 1 decimal place.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>14.5 + \dfrac{6}{9} \times 10 = 21.2</m> minutes. Since <m>10 = 40 \div 4</m>, this is the lower quartile. Point out the strip in <xref ref="fig-quartile-interpolation-strip"/>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>In the same way, estimate the times of the 20th and 30th learners.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>20th: <m>24.5 + \dfrac{20 - 13}{13} \times 10 = 29.9</m> minutes (the median). 30th: <m>34.5 + \dfrac{30 - 26}{8} \times 10 = 39.5</m> minutes (the upper quartile).</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <figure xml:id="fig-quartile-interpolation-strip">
            <caption>The class 15–24 as a strip from <m>14.5</m> to <m>24.5</m> minutes, cut into <m>9</m> equal cells, one for each learner. The 10th learner overall is the 6th in the class.</caption>
            <image width="60%">
                <shortdescription>A strip from 14.5 to 24.5 minutes in 9 equal cells, the first 6 shaded. A note on the left says 4 learners come before 14.5. An arrow two thirds of the way along marks Q1, about 21.2 minutes.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm, y=1cm]
\foreach \i in {0,...,8} {
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\a}{\i*10/9}
  \pgfmathsetmacro{\b}{(\i+1)*10/9}
  \ifnum\i&lt;6 \fill[gray!35] (\a,0) rectangle (\b,1); \fi
  \draw (\a,0) rectangle (\b,1);
}
\node[below] at (0,0) {14.5};
\node[below] at (10,0) {24.5};
\node[left, align=right, font=\small] at (-0.2,0.5) {4 learners\\before 14.5};
\draw[->, thick] (6.667,2) -- (6.667,1.08);
\node[above] at (6.667,2) {$Q_1 \approx 21.2$ min};
\node[below, font=\small] at (5,-0.5) {time (minutes)};
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise the formula for the median and quartiles of grouped data.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (c) is the formula: start at the lower class boundary and go the fraction <m>6/9</m> of the class width.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>L</m> is the lower class boundary, not the class limit; <m>CF</m> is the cumulative frequency before the class.</p></li>
                    <li><p>For grouped data use <m>n/4</m>, <m>n/2</m> and <m>3n/4</m>, not the <m>n + 1</m> positions used for lists.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-grouped-quartiles">
            <title>Quartiles of Grouped Data</title>
            <p>
                For grouped data, the median is at position <m>n/2</m>, the <term>lower quartile</term> <m>Q_1</m> at <m>n/4</m> and the <term>upper quartile</term> <m>Q_3</m> at <m>3n/4</m>. <m>Q_1</m> has a quarter of the data at or below it. Find the class that holds the position (the first class whose cumulative frequency reaches it). Inside a class we assume the values are spread evenly from the lower class boundary to the upper, so
                <md>Q = L + \frac{\text{position} - CF}{F} \times c</md>
                where <m>L</m> is the lower class boundary of that class, <m>CF</m> the cumulative frequency before it, <m>F</m> its frequency and <m>c</m> the class width.
            </p>
            <p>
                There is no <m>+1</m> here: with grouped data a position is a point on a continuous scale, not the number of a value in a list, and for large <m>n</m> the difference is very small.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-goats-quartiles">
            <title>Goats: Quartiles by Calculation</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Estimate the median and the quartiles of the masses of the <m>50</m> goats at Garissa market in <xref ref="example-goats-ogive"/>.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-goats-quartiles">
                    <title>Masses of 50 Goats</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Mass (kg)</cell><cell>15–19</cell><cell>20–24</cell><cell>25–29</cell><cell>30–34</cell><cell>35–39</cell><cell>40–44</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>7</cell><cell>12</cell><cell>14</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>5</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Cumulative frequency (cf)</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>22</cell><cell>36</cell><cell>45</cell><cell>50</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><alert>Step 1: positions.</alert> <m>n = 50</m>, so <m>Q_1</m> is at <m>50 \div 4 = 12.5</m>, the median at <m>25</m> and <m>Q_3</m> at <m>37.5</m>.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 2: <m>Q_1</m>.</alert> The cumulative frequency first reaches <m>12.5</m> in the class 25–29, so <m>L = 24.5</m>, <m>CF = 10</m>, <m>F = 12</m>, <m>c = 5</m>:
                    <md>Q_1 = 24.5 + \frac{12.5 - 10}{12} \times 5 = 25.54\ldots \approx 25.5 \,\text{kg}</md></p>
                <p><alert>Step 3: median.</alert> Position <m>25</m> is in 30–34: <m>L = 29.5</m>, <m>CF = 22</m>, <m>F = 14</m>:
                    <md>\text{median} = 29.5 + \frac{25 - 22}{14} \times 5 = 30.57\ldots \approx 30.6 \,\text{kg}</md></p>
                <p><alert>Step 4: <m>Q_3</m>.</alert> Position <m>37.5</m> is in 35–39: <m>L = 34.5</m>, <m>CF = 36</m>, <m>F = 9</m>:
                    <md>Q_3 = 34.5 + \frac{37.5 - 36}{9} \times 5 = 35.33\ldots \approx 35.3 \,\text{kg}</md></p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-bank-quartiles">
            <title>Bank Waiting Times</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Estimate the median and quartiles of the waiting times of the <m>50</m> customers at a bank in Kisumu in <xref ref="example-bank-ogive"/>: frequencies <m>6, 14, 18, 8, 4</m> for <m>0 \le t \lt 5</m>, <m>5 \le t \lt 10</m>, <m>10 \le t \lt 15</m>, <m>15 \le t \lt 20</m> and <m>20 \le t \lt 25</m> minutes.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>These classes are written with their boundaries, so <m>L</m> is the number written. The cumulative frequencies are <m>6, 20, 38, 46, 50</m>, and the positions are <m>12.5</m>, <m>25</m> and <m>37.5</m>.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>Q_1 \amp = 5 + \frac{12.5 - 6}{14} \times 5 \approx 7.3 \text{ minutes}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\text{median} \amp = 10 + \frac{25 - 20}{18} \times 5 \approx 11.4 \text{ minutes}</mrow>
                        <mrow>Q_3 \amp = 10 + \frac{37.5 - 20}{18} \times 5 \approx 14.9 \text{ minutes}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The median and <m>Q_3</m> are in the same class: that is fine, because the class 10 to 15 minutes holds <m>18</m> customers.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-missing-frequency-median">
            <title>A Missing Frequency</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The lengths, in cm (to the nearest cm), of fish landed at a beach in Homa Bay are shown below. The median length is <m>37\,\text{cm}</m>. Find <m>x</m>.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-missing-frequency-median">
                    <title>Lengths of Fish</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Length (cm)</cell><cell>10–19</cell><cell>20–29</cell><cell>30–39</cell><cell>40–49</cell><cell>50–59</cell><cell>60–69</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>14</cell><cell>12</cell><cell><m>x</m></cell><cell>4</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>A median of <m>37\,\text{cm}</m> lies in the class 30–39, so <m>L = 29.5</m>, <m>CF = 5 + 9 = 14</m>, <m>F = 14</m> and <m>c = 10</m>. The unknown <m>x</m> comes after this class, so it changes <m>n = 44 + x</m> but not <m>CF</m> or <m>F</m>.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>37 \amp = 29.5 + \frac{(44 + x)/2 - 14}{14} \times 10</mrow>
                        <mrow>10.5 \amp = (44 + x)/2 - 14</mrow>
                        <mrow>x \amp = 5</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Check: <m>n = 49</m>, and position <m>24.5</m> does lie in 30–39 (cumulative frequencies <m>14</m> and <m>28</m>).</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>30.6</c>. Give answers to the accuracy asked for.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-quartiles-of-grouped-data">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-quartiles-of-grouped-data" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/03-quartiles-deciles-and-percentiles-for-grouped-data/Quartiles-of-grouped-data.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-finding-the-mistake-in-a-grouped-quartile">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-finding-the-mistake-in-a-grouped-quartile" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/03-quartiles-deciles-and-percentiles-for-grouped-data/Finding-the-mistake-in-a-grouped-quartile.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-finding-a-missing-frequency-from-the-median">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-finding-a-missing-frequency-from-the-median" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/03-quartiles-deciles-and-percentiles-for-grouped-data/Finding-a-missing-frequency-from-the-median.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-grouped-quartiles">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-quartiles-seedlings">
                <statement><p>Use the heights of the <m>60</m> seedlings in <xref ref="ex-ogive-seedlings"/> (cumulative frequencies <m>4, 13, 28, 46, 56, 60</m> for the classes 10–14 to 35–39 cm). Calculate the lower quartile, the median and the upper quartile.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>Q_1 \approx 20.2\,\text{cm}</m>, median <m>\approx 25.1\,\text{cm}</m>, <m>Q_3 \approx 29.2\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Positions <m>15</m>, <m>30</m>, <m>45</m>. <m>Q_1 = 19.5 + \dfrac{15 - 13}{15} \times 5 = 20.17</m>; median <m>= 24.5 + \dfrac{30 - 28}{18} \times 5 = 25.06</m>; <m>Q_3 = 24.5 + \dfrac{45 - 28}{18} \times 5 = 29.22</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-quartiles-rainfall">
                <statement><p>Use the Kericho rainfall data in <xref ref="ex-ogive-rainfall"/> (frequencies <m>5, 8, 12, 9, 4, 2</m> for <m>0 \le r \lt 10</m> to <m>50 \le r \lt 60\,\text{mm}</m>). Calculate the quartiles and the median.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>Q_1 \approx 16.3\,\text{mm}</m>, median <m>\approx 25.8\,\text{mm}</m>, <m>Q_3 \approx 35.6\,\text{mm}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Cumulative frequencies <m>5, 13, 25, 34, 38, 40</m>; positions <m>10, 20, 30</m>. <m>Q_1 = 10 + \dfrac{10 - 5}{8} \times 10 = 16.25</m>; median <m>= 20 + \dfrac{20 - 13}{12} \times 10 = 25.83</m>; <m>Q_3 = 30 + \dfrac{30 - 25}{9} \times 10 = 35.56</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-quartiles-tomatoes">
                <statement>
                    <p>The masses, in g (to the nearest g), of <m>60</m> tomatoes from a farm in Kirinyaga are shown below. Find the median and the quartiles.</p>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Mass (g)</cell><cell>50–59</cell><cell>60–69</cell><cell>70–79</cell><cell>80–89</cell><cell>90–99</cell><cell>100–109</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>6</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>17</cell><cell>13</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>6</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </statement>
                <answer><p><m>Q_1 = 68.5\,\text{g}</m>, median <m>\approx 77.7\,\text{g}</m>, <m>Q_3 \approx 88.7\,\text{g}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Cumulative frequencies <m>6, 16, 33, 46, 54, 60</m>; positions <m>15, 30, 45</m>. <m>Q_1 = 59.5 + \dfrac{15 - 6}{10} \times 10 = 68.5</m>; median <m>= 69.5 + \dfrac{30 - 16}{17} \times 10 = 77.74</m>; <m>Q_3 = 79.5 + \dfrac{45 - 33}{13} \times 10 = 88.73</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-wafula-lower-quartile">
                <statement><p>Wafula found the lower quartile of the seedling heights in <xref ref="ex-quartiles-seedlings"/> as <m>20.7\,\text{cm}</m>. What was his mistake?</p></statement>
                <answer><p>He used the class limit <m>L = 20</m> instead of the lower class boundary <m>19.5</m>; the correct value is <m>20.2\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>20 + \dfrac{15 - 13}{15} \times 5 = 20.67 \approx 20.7</m>. The heights are to the nearest cm, so the class 20–24 starts at <m>19.5</m>, which gives <m>20.17 \approx 20.2\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-why-not-n-plus-one">
                <statement><p>Use position <m>(n + 1)/4</m> instead of <m>n/4</m> to find <m>Q_1</m> for the seedlings. Compare with your answer to <xref ref="ex-quartiles-seedlings"/>. Why does this book use <m>n/4</m> for grouped data?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>20.25\,\text{cm}</m> against <m>20.17\,\text{cm}</m>: a very small difference. With grouped data a position is a point on a continuous scale, not the number of a value in a list.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>Position <m>61/4 = 15.25</m>: <m>19.5 + \dfrac{15.25 - 13}{15} \times 5 = 20.25\,\text{cm}</m>. The individual values are not known, so there is no 15th value to find; we are cutting a continuous scale at a quarter of the total frequency, which is <m>n/4</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-missing-frequency-in-median-class">
                <title>Challenge: A Missing Frequency in the Median Class</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>The times, in minutes, that <m>4 + 9 + x + 10 + 5</m> learners took to finish a puzzle are grouped as <m>0 \le t \lt 10</m>, <m>10 \le t \lt 20</m>, <m>20 \le t \lt 30</m>, <m>30 \le t \lt 40</m> and <m>40 \le t \lt 50</m>, with frequencies <m>4, 9, x, 10, 5</m>. The median is <m>26</m> minutes and lies in the class <m>20 \le t \lt 30</m>. Find <m>x</m>.</p>
                </statement>
                <hint><p><m>x</m> appears twice: in <m>n</m> and as the frequency <m>F</m> of the median class.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>x = 10</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>n = 28 + x</m>, <m>L = 20</m>, <m>CF = 13</m>, <m>F = x</m>: <m>26 = 20 + \dfrac{(28 + x)/2 - 13}{x} \times 10</m>. So <m>6x = 10\left(\dfrac{28 + x}{2} - 13\right) = 5x + 10</m>, giving <m>x = 10</m>. Check: <m>n = 38</m>, position <m>19</m> lies between the cumulative frequencies <m>13</m> and <m>23</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-grouped-quartiles">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
