    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 3.2 (Core) -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-deciles-and-percentiles-grouped" component="core">
        <title>Deciles and Percentiles of Grouped Data</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the decile <m>D_k</m> is at position <m>kn/10</m> and the percentile <m>P_k</m> at <m>kn/100</m>; the same formula as for quartiles.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> estimate deciles and percentiles of grouped data, and the percentage of values below a given value.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> find cut-off marks and the spread of the middle 80 per cent.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find a cut-off mark for a prize, which is a percentile.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Ask: <q>The county gives certificates to the top 10 per cent of learners. What mark is the cut-off?</q> Let groups decide how many learners that is before they look for the mark.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-top-ten-percent">
            <title>Top Ten Percent</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The marks out of 100 of <m>80</m> learners in a county mock examination are shown below.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-county-mock-marks">
                    <title>Marks of 80 Learners</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Mark</cell><cell>20–29</cell><cell>30–39</cell><cell>40–49</cell><cell>50–59</cell><cell>60–69</cell><cell>70–79</cell><cell>80–89</cell><cell>90–99</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>6</cell><cell>11</cell><cell>18</cell><cell>19</cell><cell>12</cell><cell>7</cell><cell>4</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>How many learners are in the top <m>10</m> per cent?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>10\%</m> of <m>80</m> is <m>8</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>So how many learners are below the cut-off mark? In which class is the cut-off?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>72</m>. The cumulative frequencies are <m>3, 9, 20, 38, 57, 69, 76, 80</m>, so position <m>72</m> is in the class 80–89.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Estimate the cut-off mark, as for a quartile.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>79.5 + \dfrac{72 - 69}{7} \times 10 = 83.8</m>. Learners with more than about <m>84</m> marks get a certificate.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Estimate the mark below which the bottom <m>10</m> per cent of learners lie.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Position <m>8</m>, in 30–39: <m>29.5 + \dfrac{8 - 3}{6} \times 10 = 37.8</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To name deciles and percentiles and connect them with quartiles.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (c) found <m>P_{90}</m>, also called <m>D_9</m>; part (d) found <m>D_1</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Only the position changes: <m>kn/10</m> for a decile, <m>kn/100</m> for a percentile. The formula is the one used for quartiles.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A common slip is to use position <m>k</m> itself instead of <m>k</m> per cent of <m>n</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-grouped-deciles-percentiles">
            <title>Deciles and Percentiles</title>
            <p>
                The <term>deciles</term> <m>D_1, D_2, \ldots, D_9</m> cut the data into tenths, and the <term>percentiles</term> <m>P_1, \ldots, P_{99}</m> into hundredths. For grouped data, <m>D_k</m> is at position <m>kn/10</m> and <m>P_k</m> at <m>kn/100</m>; then use the same formula
                <md>L + \frac{\text{position} - CF}{F} \times c</md>.
                So <m>D_5 = P_{50} = Q_2</m>, the median, and <m>P_{25} = Q_1</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Working backwards, the <term>percentile rank</term> of a value is the percentage of the data below it.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-county-mock-deciles">
            <title>County Mock Marks</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use the county mock marks in <xref ref="act-top-ten-percent"/> to find <m>D_1</m> and <m>D_9</m>, and describe the middle <m>80</m> per cent of learners.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>D_1</m> is at position <m>1 \times 80 \div 10 = 8</m>, in 30–39 (<m>CF = 3</m>, <m>F = 6</m>); <m>D_9</m> is at <m>72</m>, in 80–89 (<m>CF = 69</m>, <m>F = 7</m>).
                    <md>
                        <mrow>D_1 \amp = 29.5 + \frac{8 - 3}{6} \times 10 \approx 37.8</mrow>
                        <mrow>D_9 \amp = 79.5 + \frac{72 - 69}{7} \times 10 \approx 83.8</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The middle <m>80</m> per cent of learners scored between about <m>38</m> and <m>84</m> marks.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-water-percentiles">
            <title>Household Water Use</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The water used in a day, <m>w</m> litres, by <m>90</m> households in Machakos is shown below. Find <m>P_{10}</m> and <m>P_{90}</m>, and the 10 to 90 percentile range.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-water-use">
                    <title>Water Used by 90 Households</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Water (litres)</cell><cell><m>0 \le w \lt 20</m></cell><cell><m>20 \le w \lt 40</m></cell><cell><m>40 \le w \lt 60</m></cell><cell><m>60 \le w \lt 80</m></cell><cell><m>80 \le w \lt 100</m></cell><cell><m>100 \le w \lt 120</m></cell><cell><m>120 \le w \lt 140</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>15</cell><cell>24</cell><cell>20</cell><cell>13</cell><cell>7</cell><cell>3</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The cumulative frequencies are <m>8, 23, 47, 67, 80, 87, 90</m>. <m>P_{10}</m> is at <m>10 \times 90 \div 100 = 9</m> and <m>P_{90}</m> at <m>81</m>.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>P_{10} \amp = 20 + \frac{9 - 8}{15} \times 20 \approx 21.3 \,\text{litres}</mrow>
                        <mrow>P_{90} \amp = 100 + \frac{81 - 80}{7} \times 20 \approx 102.9 \,\text{litres}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The 10 to 90 percentile range is <m>102.86 - 21.33 \approx 81.5\,\text{litres}</m>: the middle <m>80</m> per cent of households used amounts spread over about <m>81.5\,\text{litres}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-water-percentile-rank">
            <title>What Percentage Used Less Than 50 Litres?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>For the households in <xref ref="example-water-percentiles"/>, estimate the percentage that used less than <m>50\,\text{litres}</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><m>50\,\text{litres}</m> is halfway through the class <m>40 \le w \lt 60</m>, which holds <m>24</m> households, with <m>23</m> before it. So about <m>23 + 24 \times \dfrac{50 - 40}{20} = 35</m> households used less than <m>50\,\text{litres}</m>, and <m>\dfrac{35}{90} \times 100 \approx 38.9\%</m>. The percentile rank of <m>50\,\text{litres}</m> is about <m>39</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>30.6</c>. Give answers to the accuracy asked for.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-deciles-and-percentiles-of-grouped-data">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-deciles-and-percentiles-of-grouped-data" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/03-quartiles-deciles-and-percentiles-for-grouped-data/Deciles-and-percentiles-of-grouped-data.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-grouped-deciles-percentiles">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-deciles-goats">
                <statement><p>Find <m>D_3</m> and <m>D_7</m> for the goat masses in <xref ref="example-goats-quartiles"/>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>D_3 \approx 26.6\,\text{kg}</m>, <m>D_7 \approx 34.1\,\text{kg}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Positions <m>15</m> and <m>35</m>. <m>D_3 = 24.5 + \dfrac{15 - 10}{12} \times 5 = 26.58</m>; <m>D_7 = 29.5 + \dfrac{35 - 22}{14} \times 5 = 34.14</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-percentiles-goats">
                <statement><p>Find <m>P_{15}</m> and <m>P_{85}</m> for the goat masses.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>P_{15} \approx 22.7\,\text{kg}</m>, <m>P_{85} \approx 38.1\,\text{kg}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Positions <m>7.5</m> and <m>42.5</m>. <m>P_{15} = 19.5 + \dfrac{7.5 - 3}{7} \times 5 = 22.71</m>; <m>P_{85} = 34.5 + \dfrac{42.5 - 36}{9} \times 5 = 38.11</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-deciles-seedlings">
                <statement><p>Find <m>D_2</m>, <m>D_8</m> and <m>P_{95}</m> for the seedling heights in <xref ref="ex-quartiles-seedlings"/>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>D_2 \approx 18.9\,\text{cm}</m>, <m>D_8 = 30.5\,\text{cm}</m>, <m>P_{95} \approx 35.8\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Positions <m>12</m>, <m>48</m>, <m>57</m>. <m>D_2 = 14.5 + \dfrac{12 - 4}{9} \times 5 = 18.94</m>; <m>D_8 = 29.5 + \dfrac{48 - 46}{10} \times 5 = 30.5</m>; <m>P_{95} = 34.5 + \dfrac{57 - 56}{4} \times 5 = 35.75</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-percentage-shorter-than-30">
                <statement><p>Estimate the percentage of the seedlings that are shorter than <m>30\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>About <m>78.3\%</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>30</m> is <m>0.5\,\text{cm}</m> into the class 30–34 (boundaries <m>29.5</m> to <m>34.5</m>), which holds <m>10</m> seedlings with <m>46</m> before it: <m>46 + 10 \times \dfrac{0.5}{5} = 47</m> seedlings, and <m>\dfrac{47}{60} \times 100 \approx 78.3\%</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-njoroge-percentile">
                <statement><p>Njoroge found <m>P_{15}</m> of the goat masses as <m>26.6\,\text{kg}</m>. What was his mistake?</p></statement>
                <answer><p>He used position <m>15</m> instead of <m>15\%</m> of <m>50</m>, which is <m>7.5</m>. The correct value is <m>22.7\,\text{kg}</m>.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>Position <m>15</m> gives <m>24.5 + \dfrac{15 - 10}{12} \times 5 = 26.6</m>, which is <m>D_3</m>. <m>P_{15}</m> is at <m>15 \times 50 \div 100 = 7.5</m>, giving <m>22.7\,\text{kg}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-deciles-and-quartiles-agree">
                <title>Challenge: Deciles, Percentiles and Quartiles</title>
                <statement><p>Show that, for any grouped frequency table, <m>D_5 = P_{50} = Q_2</m> and <m>P_{75} = Q_3</m>.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Compare the positions.</p></hint>
                <answer><p>The positions are equal, so the values are equal.</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>D_5</m> is at <m>5n/10 = n/2</m>, <m>P_{50}</m> at <m>50n/100 = n/2</m> and <m>Q_2</m> at <m>n/2</m>. <m>P_{75}</m> is at <m>75n/100 = 3n/4</m>, the position of <m>Q_3</m>. The same position in the same table gives the same value.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-grouped-deciles-percentiles">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Provide a table of positions <m>kn/10</m> already worked out for <m>n = 50</m> and <m>n = 60</m>. Assign <xref ref="ex-deciles-goats"/> and <xref ref="ex-percentiles-goats"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-deciles-goats"/> to <xref ref="ex-njoroge-percentile"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-deciles-and-quartiles-agree"/>. Extension: find <m>P_{99}</m> of the county mock marks and say why it is unreliable (answer: position <m>79.2</m> in the last class, which holds only <m>4</m> learners: <m>89.5 + \dfrac{3.2}{4} \times 10 = 97.5</m>; it depends on how just four marks are spread).</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 3.3 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-estimating-from-the-ogive">
        <title>Estimating from a Cumulative Frequency Curve</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> a quantile is read by going across from its position on the cumulative frequency axis to the curve, then down.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> read the median and quartiles from a cumulative frequency curve, and find the interquartile range and quartile deviation from them.</p></li>
                <li component="core"><p><alert>Do (Core):</alert> read deciles and percentiles from the curve.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> estimate how many values lie above or below a given value.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners read values off their own cumulative frequency curve and compare them with the values they calculated.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Learners use the graph they drew in the activity of the previous section. Learners who joined their points with a smooth curve will get slightly different readings; ask them why.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-reading-our-curve">
            <title>Reading Our Curve</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use your cumulative frequency curve of the journey times from <xref ref="act-getting-to-school"/>.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Go across from <m>10</m>, <m>20</m> and <m>30</m> on the cumulative frequency axis to your graph, then down to the time axis. Write down the three times.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>About <m>21</m>, <m>30</m> and <m>39.5</m> minutes.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Compare your readings with the values you calculated in <xref ref="act-where-is-the-10th-learner"/>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>With straight segments they agree (<m>21.2</m>, <m>29.9</m>, <m>39.5</m>): the straight segment across a class is exactly the even spread the formula assumes. A smooth curve gives slightly different readings.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Use your readings to find the difference between the upper and lower quartiles, and half of it.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>39.5 - 21.2 = 18.3</m> minutes and <m>9.2</m> minutes (from the unrounded <m>39.5 - 21.17 = 18.33</m>). Name them now: the interquartile range and the quartile deviation.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li component="core">
                            <p>Go across from <m>36</m>. Which decile is this, and what is its value?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>36 = 9 \times 40 \div 10</m>, so this is <m>D_9</m>: <m>44.5 + \dfrac{36 - 34}{4} \times 10 = 49.5</m> minutes.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise reading from the curve, and the interquartile range and quartile deviation.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> across at the position, then down to the value; the answer is a value of the data, not a cumulative frequency.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The interquartile range is the spread of the middle half of the data; the quartile deviation is half of it.</p></li>
                    <li><p>To count values above a given value, go up from it, across to the cumulative frequency, and subtract from <m>n</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-reading-the-curve">
            <title>Reading the Curve</title>
            <p>
                To estimate a median or quartile from a cumulative frequency curve, find its position (<m>n/2</m>, <m>n/4</m> or <m>3n/4</m>) on the cumulative frequency axis, go across to the curve, then down to the horizontal axis.
            </p>
            <p>
                The <term>interquartile range</term> is <m>Q_3 - Q_1</m>, the spread of the middle half of the data. The <term>quartile deviation</term> (or semi-interquartile range) is half of it, <m>\dfrac{Q_3 - Q_1}{2}</m>.
            </p>
            <p component="core">
                Deciles and percentiles are read in the same way, going across from <m>kn/10</m> or <m>kn/100</m>.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ogive-percentile-reader">
            <caption>Drag the slider to choose a percentage. A line goes across from that percentage of the <m>50</m> goats to the cumulative frequency curve, then down to the mass axis.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-ogive-percentile-reader" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/ogive-percentile-reader.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-ogive-percentile-reader" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>The graph is the cumulative frequency curve of the goat masses from <xref ref="example-goats-ogive"/>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Press the <alert>25%</alert>, <alert>50%</alert> and <alert>75%</alert> buttons to read <m>Q_1</m>, the median and <m>Q_3</m>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Drag the <alert>p</alert> slider and watch the reading change. Where does a small change in <m>p</m> make the biggest change in the mass?</p></li>
                            <li component="core"><p>Set <alert>p</alert> to <m>10, 20, \ldots, 90</m> to read the deciles.</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-goats-quartiles-from-curve">
            <title>Goats: Quartiles from the Curve</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use the cumulative frequency curve of the goat masses (<xref ref="fig-goats-ogive"/>) to estimate the median, the quartiles, the interquartile range and the quartile deviation.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>With <m>n = 50</m>, go across from <m>12.5</m>, <m>25</m> and <m>37.5</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-goats-ogive-quartiles">
                    <caption>Reading the quartiles of the goat masses from the curve.</caption>
                    <image width="90%">
                        <shortdescription>The goat-mass cumulative frequency curve with dashed lines across from 12.5, 25 and 37.5 to the curve and down to the mass axis at about 25.5, 30.6 and 35.3 kg.</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Horizontal axis mass in kg from 10 to 50; vertical axis cumulative frequency from 0 to 50. Plotted points (14.5, 0), (19.5, 3), (24.5, 10), (29.5, 22), (34.5, 36), (39.5, 45), (44.5, 50) joined by straight line segments. Dashed lines go across from cumulative frequency 12.5, 25 and 37.5 to the graph and down to the mass axis at 25.5, 30.6 and 35.3 kg.</p></description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=12cm, height=8cm, xmin=10, xmax=50, ymin=0, ymax=52,
  xtick={10,15,...,50}, ytick={0,10,...,50}, minor x tick num=4, minor y tick num=4,
  grid=both, major grid style={gray!50}, minor grid style={gray!20},
  xlabel={Mass (kg)}, ylabel={Cumulative frequency}]
\addplot[thick, mark=*] coordinates {(14.5,0) (19.5,3) (24.5,10) (29.5,22) (34.5,36) (39.5,45) (44.5,50)};
\addplot[dashed, thick] coordinates {(10,12.5) (25.54,12.5) (25.54,0)};
\addplot[dashed, thick] coordinates {(10,25) (30.57,25) (30.57,0)};
\addplot[dashed, thick] coordinates {(10,37.5) (35.33,37.5) (35.33,0)};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (axis cs:10.2,12.5) {12.5};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (axis cs:10.2,25) {25};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (axis cs:10.2,37.5) {37.5};
\node[anchor=south, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:25.54,3) {25.5};
\node[anchor=south, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:30.57,6) {30.6};
\node[anchor=south, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:35.33,9) {35.3};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p><m>Q_1 \approx 25.5\,\text{kg}</m>, median <m>\approx 30.6\,\text{kg}</m> and <m>Q_3 \approx 35.3\,\text{kg}</m>, the same as the values calculated in <xref ref="example-goats-quartiles"/>. So the interquartile range is about <m>35.33 - 25.54 = 9.8\,\text{kg}</m> and the quartile deviation about <m>4.9\,\text{kg}</m>. The middle half of the goats weigh between about <m>25.5</m> and <m>35.3\,\text{kg}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-goats-deciles-from-curve" component="core">
            <title>Goats: Deciles and Percentiles from the Curve</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use the curve to estimate <m>D_1</m>, <m>D_9</m> and <m>P_{80}</m> of the goat masses.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Go across from <m>50 \div 10 = 5</m>, <m>45</m> and <m>80 \times 50 \div 100 = 40</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-goats-ogive-deciles">
                    <caption>Reading <m>D_1</m>, <m>P_{80}</m> and <m>D_9</m> of the goat masses from the curve.</caption>
                    <image width="90%">
                        <shortdescription>The goat-mass cumulative frequency curve with dashed lines across from 5, 40 and 45 to the curve and down to the mass axis at about 20.9, 36.7 and 39.5 kg.</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Horizontal axis mass in kg from 10 to 50; vertical axis cumulative frequency from 0 to 50. The same points as the goat-mass curve, joined by straight line segments. Dashed lines go across from cumulative frequency 5, 40 and 45 to the graph and down to the mass axis at 20.9, 36.7 and 39.5 kg.</p></description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=12cm, height=8cm, xmin=10, xmax=50, ymin=0, ymax=52,
  xtick={10,15,...,50}, ytick={0,10,...,50}, minor x tick num=4, minor y tick num=4,
  grid=both, major grid style={gray!50}, minor grid style={gray!20},
  xlabel={Mass (kg)}, ylabel={Cumulative frequency}]
\addplot[thick, mark=*] coordinates {(14.5,0) (19.5,3) (24.5,10) (29.5,22) (34.5,36) (39.5,45) (44.5,50)};
\addplot[dashed, thick] coordinates {(10,5) (20.93,5) (20.93,0)};
\addplot[dashed, thick] coordinates {(10,40) (36.72,40) (36.72,0)};
\addplot[dashed, thick] coordinates {(10,45) (39.5,45) (39.5,0)};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (axis cs:10.2,5) {5};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (axis cs:10.2,40) {40};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (axis cs:10.2,45) {45};
\node[anchor=south, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:20.93,1) {20.9};
\node[anchor=south, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:36.72,3) {36.7};
\node[anchor=south, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:39.5,7) {39.5};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p><m>D_1 \approx 20.9\,\text{kg}</m>, <m>P_{80} \approx 36.7\,\text{kg}</m> and <m>D_9 = 39.5\,\text{kg}</m>. By calculation, for example, <m>P_{80} = 34.5 + \dfrac{40 - 36}{9} \times 5 = 36.72</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-goats-heavier-than-32">
            <title>Heavier Than 32 kg</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use the goat-mass curve.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Estimate how many of the goats weigh more than <m>32\,\text{kg}</m>.</p></li>
                        <li component="core"><p>Above what mass are the heaviest <m>20</m> per cent of the goats?</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>(a) Go up from <m>32\,\text{kg}</m> to the curve, then across: the cumulative frequency is <m>22 + 14 \times \dfrac{32 - 29.5}{5} = 29</m>. So <m>29</m> goats weigh less than <m>32\,\text{kg}</m> and about <m>50 - 29 = 21</m> goats weigh more.</p>
                <p component="core">(b) The heaviest <m>20</m> per cent are above <m>P_{80} \approx 36.7\,\text{kg}</m> (<xref ref="example-goats-deciles-from-curve"/>).</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <p>
            <em>Statistics in everyday life:</em> a county education office uses the quartiles of mock marks to decide which schools need extra support. Where else might a cut-off read from a cumulative frequency curve be used?
        </p>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>30.6</c>. Give answers to the accuracy asked for.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-reading-quartiles-from-a-cumulative-frequency-curve">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-reading-quartiles-from-a-cumulative-frequency-curve" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/03-quartiles-deciles-and-percentiles-for-grouped-data/Reading-quartiles-from-a-cumulative-frequency-curve.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-reading-percentiles-from-a-cumulative-frequency-curve" component="core">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-reading-percentiles-from-a-cumulative-frequency-curve" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/03-quartiles-deciles-and-percentiles-for-grouped-data/Reading-percentiles-from-a-cumulative-frequency-curve.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-estimating-from-the-curve">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-curve-seedlings-quartiles">
                <statement><p>Use your curve from <xref ref="ex-ogive-seedlings"/> to estimate the median, the quartiles, the interquartile range and the quartile deviation of the seedling heights.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Median <m>\approx 25.1\,\text{cm}</m>, <m>Q_1 \approx 20.2\,\text{cm}</m>, <m>Q_3 \approx 29.2\,\text{cm}</m>; IQR <m>\approx 9.1\,\text{cm}</m>; QD <m>\approx 4.5\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Go across from <m>30</m>, <m>15</m> and <m>45</m>. The readings match <xref ref="ex-quartiles-seedlings"/>: IQR <m>= 29.22 - 20.17 = 9.06 \approx 9.1\,\text{cm}</m> and QD <m>\approx 4.5\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-curve-rainfall-spread">
                <statement><p>Use your curve from <xref ref="ex-ogive-rainfall"/> to estimate the median, the interquartile range and the quartile deviation of the rainfall.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Median <m>\approx 25.8\,\text{mm}</m>; IQR <m>\approx 19.3\,\text{mm}</m>; QD <m>\approx 9.7\,\text{mm}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Across from <m>20</m>, <m>10</m> and <m>30</m>: <m>25.8</m>, <m>16.25</m> and <m>35.56\,\text{mm}</m>. IQR <m>= 35.56 - 16.25 = 19.31</m>; QD <m>= 9.65 \approx 9.7\,\text{mm}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-curve-seedlings-deciles" component="core">
                <statement><p>Use the seedling curve to estimate <m>D_2</m> and <m>D_8</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>D_2 \approx 18.9\,\text{cm}</m>, <m>D_8 \approx 30.5\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-curve-taller-than-27">
                <statement><p>Use the seedling curve to estimate how many seedlings are taller than <m>27\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>About <m>23</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Up from <m>27</m> and across: about <m>37</m> seedlings are shorter than <m>27\,\text{cm}</m>, so about <m>60 - 37 = 23</m> are taller.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-curve-water-percentiles" component="core">
                <statement><p>Draw the cumulative frequency curve of the household water use in <xref ref="example-water-percentiles"/>, and read <m>P_{10}</m> and <m>P_{90}</m> from it.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>P_{10} \approx 21.3\,\text{litres}</m>, <m>P_{90} \approx 102.9\,\text{litres}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Plot <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(20, 8)</m>, <m>(40, 23)</m>, <m>(60, 47)</m>, <m>(80, 67)</m>, <m>(100, 80)</m>, <m>(120, 87)</m>, <m>(140, 90)</m>. Go across from <m>9</m> and <m>81</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-why-curve-equals-calculation">
                <title>Challenge: Why the Curve Agrees with the Formula</title>
                <statement><p>Explain why a reading from a cumulative frequency curve drawn with straight segments always equals the value from the formula <m>L + \dfrac{\text{position} - CF}{F} \times c</m>.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Look at the segment across the class that holds the position, and use similar triangles.</p></hint>
                <answer><p>Across one class the segment rises <m>F</m> while it moves <m>c</m>; going up <m>\text{position} - CF</m> from the start of the class moves across <m>\dfrac{\text{position} - CF}{F} \times c</m>, which is the formula.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>The segment for the class joins <m>(L, CF)</m> to <m>(L + c, CF + F)</m>. A point on it at height <m>\text{position}</m> has risen <m>\text{position} - CF</m> out of <m>F</m>, so by similar triangles it has moved the same fraction of <m>c</m> across: its horizontal coordinate is <m>L + \dfrac{\text{position} - CF}{F} \times c</m>. A straight segment is exactly the even spread inside a class that the formula assumes.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-estimating-from-the-curve">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a printed copy of <xref ref="fig-goats-ogive"/> with the grid, and a template showing one dashed reading line (across, then down). Assign <xref ref="ex-curve-seedlings-quartiles"/> and <xref ref="ex-curve-taller-than-27"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-curve-seedlings-quartiles"/>, <xref ref="ex-curve-rainfall-spread"/> and <xref ref="ex-curve-taller-than-27"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-why-curve-equals-calculation"/>. Extension: read your curve from the activity at <m>45</m> minutes. How many learners took longer? (Answer: the cumulative frequency is about <m>34.2</m>, so about <m>6</m> learners.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>


